  If you want to do this without using a library, you can use a function that performs a deep copy of objects. Here's an example of such a function:

  function cloneObject(obj) {
      var clone = {};
      for (var i in obj) {
          if (obj[i] != null && typeof obj[i] == "object")
              clone[i] = cloneObject(obj[i]);
          else
              clone[i] = obj[i];
      }
      return clone;
  }

  This function works by iterating over the properties of the input object and recursively copying any nested objects. It handles primitive values, arrays, and objects, but it does not handle special cases like Date objects or RegExp objects. If you need to clone those, you'll have to add additional checks and handling for them.

  Keep in mind that this function will not work for objects with circular references, as it will cause an infinite loop. To handle circular references, you can use a technique called "hashing" to keep track of already cloned objects.

  Also, be aware that this function will not preserve the prototype chain of the original object. If you need to preserve the prototype chain, you can use Object.create to create a new object with the same prototype.

